        <p class="eyebrow">About this episode</p>
        <h2>Sometimes showing up is the most important thing a friend can do.</h2>
        <div class="content-note"><strong>Content note</strong>This episode includes discussion of serious illness, anticipatory grief, childhood trauma, complicated family relationships, depression, and end-of-life decisions. Please listen with care and seek support from someone you trust if these topics affect you.</div>
        <p class="lede">In the first installment of the podcast’s mental-health check-ins, Mikel and Zach are joined by their friend Josh for a vulnerable conversation about grief, family, and what it means to be present for one another.</p>
        <p>Josh shares the emotional weight of his father’s declining health and the complicated feelings that come with a difficult family history. What begins as an unexpected recording becomes an honest look at anticipatory grief, emotions that arrive without warning, and the tension between wanting relief and knowing painful feelings still need to be faced.</p>
        <p>Together, the friends discuss why emotions can feel especially difficult in male-dominated workplaces, how a simple task can create enough space to regroup, and the difference between taking a healthy step back and continually suppressing what hurts. They also reflect on faith, end-of-life uncertainty, and the limits of trying to find perfect words for someone who is grieving.</p>
        <p>By the end of the conversation, Josh describes feeling better—not because every problem has been solved, but because he did not have to carry it alone.</p>

        <h3>In this episode</h3>
        <ul class="topic-list">
          <li>Anticipatory grief when a parent’s health is declining</li>
          <li>How childhood experiences complicate family loss and forgiveness</li>
          <li>Why emotions can feel unsafe or unwelcome in some workplaces</li>
          <li>The difference between pausing to regroup and avoiding emotions indefinitely</li>
          <li>How everyday tasks can provide a needed mental reset</li>
          <li>The uncertainty families face around serious illness and end-of-life care</li>
          <li>Why listening and staying present can matter more than finding perfect advice</li>
          <li>How sharing a personal story can remind someone else they are not alone</li>
        </ul>
      </article>

